>
  <p>
    In Newton's law of universal gravitation, one number determines the strength of gravity throughout the entire
    universe: the gravitational constant G. Despite its fundamental importance, G is the least precisely measured
    of all fundamental constants, and scientists still debate why gravity is so remarkably weak.
  </p>

  <h2>What is G?</h2>
  <p>
    The gravitational constant G appears in Newton's equation: F = G × (m1 × m2) / r². It determines how much
    gravitational force exists between two masses at a given distance. G's value is approximately 6.674 × 10^-11
    cubic meters per kilogram per second squared—a tiny number reflecting gravity's weakness.
  </p>

  <h3>Universal and Unchanging</h3>
  <p>
    As far as we know, G is the same everywhere in the universe and has been constant throughout cosmic history.
    Astronomical observations and Earth-based experiments have confirmed G's constancy to high precision, though
    some theories suggest it might vary on cosmological timescales.
  </p>

  <h2>The Challenge of Measuring G</h2>
  <p>
    Unlike other fundamental constants, measuring G is extremely difficult because gravity is so weak. Electromagnetic
    forces easily overwhelm gravitational forces in experiments. Even Earth's tides, random seismic vibrations, and
    temperature changes can affect sensitive measurements.
  </p>

  <h3>The Cavendish Experiment</h3>
  <p>
    In 1798, Henry Cavendish performed the first measurement of G using a torsion balance—two small masses on a
    rod suspended by a thin fiber. Large lead spheres were brought near, and the tiny gravitational attraction
    twisted the fiber slightly. From this barely perceptible twist, Cavendish determined G to within 1% of the
    modern value.
  </p>

  <h2>Modern Measurement Challenges</h2>
  <p>
    Despite centuries of improvements, recent measurements of G disagree significantly—far more than their stated
    uncertainties would suggest. Different experimental methods yield values that differ by more than 0.05%, a
    frustrating inconsistency for such a fundamental constant.
  </p>

  <h3>Why the Discrepancies?</h3>
  <p>
    Possible explanations include:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Unaccounted systematic errors in different experimental setups</li>
    <li>Gravitational effects from surrounding matter being underestimated</li>
    <li>Quantum effects or new physics at small scales</li>
    <li>Variations in local gravitational field strength</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why is Gravity So Weak?</h2>
  <p>
    Gravity is by far the weakest fundamental force. The electromagnetic force between two protons is about 10^36
    times stronger than their gravitational attraction. You can demonstrate gravity's weakness by picking up a
    paperclip with a tiny magnet—the magnet's electromagnetic force easily overcomes Earth's entire gravitational
    pull on the paperclip.
  </p>

  <h3>Theories About Gravity's Weakness</h3>
  <p>
    Several theories attempt to explain why gravity is so weak:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Extra dimensions: Gravity might spread into extra spatial dimensions we can't see, diluting its strength</li>
    <li>Graviton properties: Gravity's quantum particle might have unique characteristics</li>
    <li>Large extra dimensions: Our universe might be a membrane in higher-dimensional space</li>
    <li>Anthropic principle: If gravity were stronger, stars would burn out too quickly for life to evolve</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The Hierarchy Problem</h2>
  <p>
    In particle physics, the huge difference between gravity's strength and other forces is called the hierarchy
    problem. Why is the Planck mass (where gravity becomes strong) so much larger than particle masses? This puzzle
    hints that our understanding of fundamental physics is incomplete.
  </p>

  <h2>G in Different Contexts</h2>

  <h3>Astronomy and Cosmology</h3>
  <p>
    Despite uncertainty in G's value, astronomical observations are incredibly precise because they measure G × M
    (mass times gravitational constant) directly. For practical astronomy, the combined quantity is more useful
    than G alone.
  </p>

  <h3>Black Holes and the Planck Scale</h3>
  <p>
    G appears in formulas for black holes and quantum gravity. The Planck length (10^-35 meters) and Planck mass
    (10^-8 kg) combine G with quantum constants, marking scales where quantum gravity effects become important.
  </p>

  <h2>Could G Change?</h2>
  <p>
    Some theories propose G might vary with time or location. Observations of ancient quasars and precise solar
    system measurements constrain any change to less than a few parts in 10^12 per year. If G changes at all,
    it does so incredibly slowly.
  </p>

  <h2>Practical Importance</h2>
  <p>
    Knowing G precisely matters for:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Calculating masses of planets and stars</li>
    <li>Understanding stellar evolution and planet formation</li>
    <li>Testing theories of gravity and cosmology</li>
    <li>Precision satellite navigation</li>
    <li>Fundamental physics research</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    <strong>Key Takeaway:</strong> The gravitational constant G quantifies gravity's strength throughout the
    universe. Despite being fundamental to physics, it's the least precisely known constant, and recent measurements
    disagree frustratingly. Understanding why gravity is so weak compared to other forces remains one of physics'
    biggest mysteries, potentially holding clues to physics beyond our current theories.
  </p>
